Why LGPL and not GPL
--------------------

A DuneOS application is a `.dap` ELF that links `libdune.a` and reaches the
kernel through a function-pointer table resolved at load time. That is linking,
not a syscall boundary. Linux can publish its kernel under plain GPL because it
carries an explicit syscall exception; DuneOS has no such exception and no MMU
to make one meaningful. Under plain GPL, every third-party `.dap` would
therefore be a derivative work and would itself have to be GPL.

LGPL draws the line where the project actually wants it: the kernel, the loader
and libdune stay copyleft — modify them and you owe your recipient the modified
sources — while an application that merely links libdune and calls the ABI
remains free to choose its own licence, proprietary included.

Copyleft compels publication to the *recipient* of a binary. It never obliges
anyone to contribute changes back to this project.


